{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he shall confess that he has sinned regarding it, and he shall bring his guilt-offering</b>. The order is not thus, but rather he brings the sin-offering and leans his hands upon it and confesses, in the manner stated further on (Leviticus 16:21): \"And Aaron shall lean both his hands upon the head of the live goat and confess over it all the iniquities of the children of Israel.\" But it did not state here \"and he shall lean his hand\" because it has already specified regarding all the sin-offerings above that they require leaning of hands, and likewise in the freewill offerings, and it did not specify confession above regarding the other sin-offerings. \n\nAccording to the plain sense, it would appear that it stated here \"and he shall confess\" because for an oath of testimony they bring a sacrifice for its intentional violation, and likewise in the oath of deposit it states (Numbers 5:7), \"and they shall confess their sin which they have done,\" but for a sin of error it does not mention confession.\n\nBut according to the opinion of our Sages (Sifrei Zuta Naso 5:5), \"and he shall confess that he has sinned regarding it\" applies to all those mentioned in the portion, including defilement of the Sanctuary and its holy things and an oath of utterance, which are done in error, and all other sins we learn from here. But it mentioned confession here because the oath of testimony and the oath of utterance do not carry karet for their intentional violation, yet they nevertheless require confession — how much more so for the definite sins above, which are liable to karet, that one must confess.\n\nAnd our Sages said that \"and he shall confess that he has sinned regarding it\" is a general principle for all sins that require confession, and the verse in the portion of Naso (Numbers 5:6–7), \"that soul is guilty and they shall confess their sin which they have done,\" serves to bring in all guilt-offerings — thus it is explicitly stated in Sifrei (Naso 2). And there they expounded: \"and that soul is guilty and they shall confess\" — a paradigm for all the dead who require confession.\n\n[The order of the verse teaches that confession precedes the offering, yet in practice one leans hands and confesses over the animal itself. For sins whose intentional violation carries only lashes, not karet, the Torah nevertheless requires confession — establishing the principle that confession is integral to every sacrificial procedure.]"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:36.779323694Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}